    "body": "Adds a config for [rust-glancer](https://github.com/rust-glancer/rust-glancer), a Rust language server that trades completeness for low memory usage and near-instant editor restarts. The repository has 348 stars.\r\n\r\nA few non-obvious things about the setup:\r\n\r\n- The project currently only distributes `.vsix` packages for VS Code, so the `rust-glancer` binary has to be built from source (`cargo build --release -p rust-glancer`) to use it with Nvim. `rust-src` is also a hard requirement (`rustup component add rust-src`).\r\n- The server only reads configuration from `initializationOptions` sent at startup; there is no `workspace/configuration` support. Settings go through `init_options`, not `settings`.\r\n- Diagnostics (`cargo check`) are disabled by default.\r\n- The default nearest-`Cargo.toml` `root_markers` resolution breaks for a crate that is a member of a Cargo workspace: it resolves to the member's own directory instead of the workspace root. rust-glancer rejects a Cargo workspace root that lies outside the LSP workspace folder it was given, so every request after `initialize` fails with `the open document could not be associated with an analysis engine`, with no indication of the cause unless `RUST_GLANCER_LOG=debug` is set. The custom `root_dir` walks upward from the buffer and picks the outermost `Cargo.toml` that declares `[workspace]`, falling back to the nearest `Cargo.toml` for standalone crates.\r\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-08-24T10:38:16Z",
